# Binary files, explicitly. Git's own "is this text" heuristic looks at the
# first few KB for a NUL byte, and a small, mostly-ASCII PDF fixture (an
# uncompressed content stream, no embedded image) reads as plain text to it —
# so with no rule here, `core.autocrlf=true` (the default a Windows Git
# install suggests) silently rewrites every `\n` inside such a file to `\r\n`
# on checkout.
#
# That is invisible for most files, and it corrupts a PDF: every byte offset
# in its cross-reference table and its `startxref` pointer is counted from
# the start of the file, so one converted newline before a pointer's target
# shifts everything after it. PDFium recovers by rescanning the file for
# objects, which is why a document still opens and renders; this crate's own
# minimal reader (`pdf_core::pdf::reader::File`) takes the declared offsets at
# their word — by design, see its own doc — and refuses a file whose `xref`
# keyword is not where `startxref` says, reporting it as an unsupported
# cross-reference *stream* (the only other legal thing at that position),
# which is a different and wrong diagnosis of what is actually a checkout
# corrupting the file underneath it.
#
# Found 2026-10-06: three `lock_wiring_tests` plus two `pdf_core::signing`
# tests (one of them reading a pre-signed fixture, where the same corruption
# risked reading as a tampering alteration rather than what it was) were
# failing from exactly this, on this machine, with nothing wrong in the code
# or the committed fixtures — confirmed by extracting the git blob directly:
# `git show` reproduces a byte-correct file every time, only the checkout is
# wrong. 11 fixtures were affected across `rust/pdf_core/fixtures` and the iOS
# resource copies.
#
# `binary` is shorthand for `-diff -merge -text`: never line-ending-converted,
# and a diff of one is reported as "Binary files differ" rather than garbage.
*.pdf binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.dll binary
*.so binary
*.dylib binary
*.exe binary
*.p12 binary
*.pfx binary
*.der binary
*.rten binary
*.zip binary
